1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,979
<font color="#000000">Satellite radar sees the ocean day and night,
through any cloud cover,</font>

2
00:00:05,059 --> 00:00:07,650
<font color="#000000">capturing wind streaks, convective cells,</font>

3
00:00:07,730 --> 00:00:11,036
<font color="#000000">and other atmospheric signatures on the sea surface.</font>

4
00:00:11,116 --> 00:00:16,573
<font color="#000000">But teaching machines to read these images is hard,
because expert labels are scarce.</font>

5
00:00:16,653 --> 00:00:22,109
<font color="#000000">This work asks whether self-supervised learning on
three million unlabeled Sentinel-1</font>

6
00:00:22,189 --> 00:00:25,430
<font color="#000000">radar images can overcome that labeling bottleneck,</font>

7
00:00:25,510 --> 00:00:27,710
<font color="#000000">and honestly reports what it found.</font>

8
00:00:28,090 --> 00:00:34,699
<font color="#000000">The European Space Agency's Sentinel-1 radar
satellites image the global ocean at unprecedented scale,</font>

9
00:00:34,779 --> 00:00:38,568
<font color="#000000">capturing waves, turbulence, fronts, and biological
slicks.</font>

10
00:00:38,648 --> 00:00:41,257
<font color="#000000">Computer vision can process these images,</font>

11
00:00:41,337 --> 00:00:45,585
<font color="#000000">but machine learning has been held back by a lack
of labeled data,</font>

12
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:49,257
<font color="#000000">since only trained experts can annotate radar vignettes.</font>

13
00:00:49,337 --> 00:00:56,799
<font color="#000000">Earlier work also relied on a biased dataset that
picked only exemplary images and forced a single label per image,</font>

14
00:00:56,879 --> 00:00:59,881
<font color="#000000">even though multiple phenomena usually coexist.</font>

15
00:00:59,961 --> 00:01:05,980
<font color="#000000">The result was a training set that did not reflect
the real distribution of ocean conditions.</font>

16
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:10,194
<font color="#000000">Contrastive self-supervised learning has
transformed computer vision by training networks</font>

17
00:01:10,274 --> 00:01:14,109
<font color="#000000">on huge pools of unlabeled data to produce
embeddings that transfer well to downstream</font>

18
00:01:14,189 --> 00:01:18,023
<font color="#000000">tasks.</font>

19
00:01:18,103 --> 00:01:21,938
<font color="#000000">It has worked for natural images and medical images,</font>

20
00:01:22,018 --> 00:01:25,852
<font color="#000000">but has rarely been applied to remote sensing.</font>

21
00:01:25,932 --> 00:01:29,767
<font color="#000000">That gap matters here,</font>

22
00:01:29,847 --> 00:01:33,681
<font color="#000000">because Sentinel-1 alone collects roughly a hundred
and twenty thousand Wave-mode ocean images every single month.</font>

23
00:01:33,761 --> 00:01:37,596
<font color="#000000">This study tests the hypothesis that contrastive
learning on that unlabeled stream can</font>

24
00:01:37,676 --> 00:01:41,510
<font color="#000000">overcome the scarcity of expert labels for SAR analysis.</font>

25
00:01:41,890 --> 00:01:45,717
<font color="#000000">This paper makes three contributions.</font>

26
00:01:45,797 --> 00:01:49,624
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

27
00:01:49,704 --> 00:01:53,531
<font color="#000000">it builds a new hand-labeled dataset of about
twenty-three hundred randomly sampled,</font>

28
00:01:53,611 --> 00:01:57,438
<font color="#000000">multi-label radar observations that better
represents the real ocean population.</font>

29
00:01:57,518 --> 00:02:01,345
<font color="#000000">Second, it leverages three years of unlabeled
Sentinel-1 imagery,</font>

30
00:02:01,425 --> 00:02:05,252
<font color="#000000">roughly three million images, to train a SwAV
contrastive embedding of SAR scenes.</font>

31
00:02:05,332 --> 00:02:09,159
<font color="#000000">Third,</font>

32
00:02:09,239 --> 00:02:13,066
<font color="#000000">it rigorously compares that self-supervised
representation against standard transfer learning</font>

33
00:02:13,146 --> 00:02:16,973
<font color="#000000">from ImageNet and against the previous
state-of-the-art CmWV classifier,</font>

34
00:02:17,053 --> 00:02:20,880
<font color="#000000">using three different downstream evaluation protocols.</font>

35
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:22,796
<font color="#000000">The core method is SwAV,</font>

36
00:02:22,876 --> 00:02:28,519
<font color="#000000">a contrastive framework that assigns image
representations to clusters and trains the</font>

37
00:02:28,599 --> 00:02:33,704
<font color="#000000">network to predict the cluster assignment of one
augmented view from another.</font>

38
00:02:33,784 --> 00:02:35,724
<font color="#000000">Unlike methods such as SimCLR,</font>

39
00:02:35,804 --> 00:02:41,716
<font color="#000000">SwAV avoids prohibitively large batches by storing
recent cluster assignments in a queue.</font>

40
00:02:41,796 --> 00:02:47,911
<font color="#000000">The authors train a standard ResNet-50 backbone
with SwAV using a batch size of one thousand</font>

41
00:02:47,991 --> 00:02:50,267
<font color="#000000">twenty-four across eight V100 GPUs,</font>

42
00:02:50,347 --> 00:02:54,509
<font color="#000000">a queue of sixteen batches, and one thousand
cluster centroids,</font>

43
00:02:54,589 --> 00:02:58,751
<font color="#000000">stopping after sixty-five epochs and about ten days
of compute.</font>

44
00:02:58,831 --> 00:03:01,040
<font color="#000000">To measure representation quality,</font>

45
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:06,292
<font color="#000000">they use three downstream protocols: a weighted
k-nearest-neighbor classifier,</font>

46
00:03:06,372 --> 00:03:11,679
<font color="#000000">a linear evaluation that freezes the backbone and
trains a single softmax layer,</font>

47
00:03:11,759 --> 00:03:16,190
<font color="#000000">and full end-to-end fine-tuning of all weights on
the labeled data.</font>

48
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:18,470
<font color="#000000">Two datasets drive the study.</font>

49
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:26,252
<font color="#000000">The pretraining pool is nearly three million
unlabeled Wave-mode images from Sentinel-1 A and B over 2017 to 2019,</font>

50
00:03:26,332 --> 00:03:31,918
<font color="#000000">each covering a twenty by twenty kilometer patch of
ocean at five meter resolution,</font>

51
00:03:31,998 --> 00:03:36,902
<font color="#000000">ninety percent used for training and ten percent
held out for validation.</font>

52
00:03:36,982 --> 00:03:42,841
<font color="#000000">The labeled benchmark contains twenty-three hundred
vignettes annotated by experts into</font>

53
00:03:42,921 --> 00:03:48,643
<font color="#000000">a consensus multi-label ground truth across four
classes: mesoscale convection cells,</font>

54
00:03:48,723 --> 00:03:51,920
<font color="#000000">wind streaks, negligible variability, and other.</font>

55
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,450
<font color="#000000">This set is stratified and split sixty, twenty,
twenty into training, validation,</font>

56
00:03:57,530 --> 00:03:59,020
<font color="#000000">and held-out test data.</font>

57
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:03,507
<font color="#000000">The headline result is that fine-tuned models reach
a best micro-averaged area under the</font>

58
00:04:03,587 --> 00:04:07,693
<font color="#000000">ROC curve of about zero point nine three.</font>

59
00:04:07,773 --> 00:04:11,880
<font color="#000000">Both the contrastive and the ImageNet-pretrained
models comfortably beat the earlier CmWV</font>

60
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:16,067
<font color="#000000">classifier on the two hardest atmospheric classes.</font>

61
00:04:16,147 --> 00:04:20,253
<font color="#000000">For wind streaks,</font>

62
00:04:20,333 --> 00:04:24,440
<font color="#000000">the new models score in the mid eighties compared
with CmWV's zero point seven three,</font>

63
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:28,627
<font color="#000000">and for convection cells they reach about zero
point eight seven versus zero point seven nine.</font>

64
00:04:28,707 --> 00:04:32,813
<font color="#000000">On the negligible-variability class, all three
models perform about equally well,</font>

65
00:04:32,893 --> 00:04:37,000
<font color="#000000">near zero point nine five.</font>

66
00:04:37,380 --> 00:04:40,894
<font color="#000000">The most informative comparison is across the three
evaluation protocols.</font>

67
00:04:40,974 --> 00:04:44,488
<font color="#000000">When the backbone is frozen,</font>

68
00:04:44,568 --> 00:04:48,083
<font color="#000000">the self-supervised contrastive weights hold a small edge,</font>

69
00:04:48,163 --> 00:04:51,677
<font color="#000000">scoring zero point eight six four versus zero point
eight five nine under nearest-neighbor</font>

70
00:04:51,757 --> 00:04:55,271
<font color="#000000">classification and zero point eight four one versus
zero point eight three six under linear</font>

71
00:04:55,351 --> 00:04:58,865
<font color="#000000">evaluation.</font>

72
00:04:58,945 --> 00:05:02,459
<font color="#000000">But once all weights are fine-tuned end to end,</font>

73
00:05:02,539 --> 00:05:06,053
<font color="#000000">that gap not only disappears but slightly reverses,</font>

74
00:05:06,133 --> 00:05:09,648
<font color="#000000">with the ImageNet initialization reaching zero
point nine three one against the contrastive</font>

75
00:05:09,728 --> 00:05:13,242
<font color="#000000">model's zero point nine two nine.</font>

76
00:05:13,322 --> 00:05:16,836
<font color="#000000">In other words,</font>

77
00:05:16,916 --> 00:05:20,430
<font color="#000000">the benefit of self-supervised pretraining vanishes
once the whole network is allowed to adapt.</font>

78
00:05:20,810 --> 00:05:22,884
<font color="#000000">A few numbers capture the study.</font>

79
00:05:22,964 --> 00:05:27,865
<font color="#000000">Self-supervised training used almost three million
unlabeled radar images.</font>

80
00:05:27,945 --> 00:05:33,587
<font color="#000000">The labeled benchmark held twenty-three hundred
expert vignettes across four classes.</font>

81
00:05:33,667 --> 00:05:39,510
<font color="#000000">The best fine-tuned model reached a micro-averaged
AUROC of about zero point nine three.</font>

82
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:43,751
<font color="#000000">And on the toughest classes, wind streaks and
convection cells,</font>

83
00:05:43,831 --> 00:05:51,357
<font color="#000000">the new models jumped from CmWV's roughly zero
point seven three and zero point seven nine into the mid eighties,</font>

84
00:05:51,437 --> 00:05:53,780
<font color="#000000">a substantial detection improvement.</font>

85
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:58,000
<font color="#000000">The honest takeaway is that self-supervised
contrastive learning,</font>

86
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:01,920
<font color="#000000">at least in this preliminary study,</font>

87
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,840
<font color="#000000">offers only marginal gains over simply transferring
features from a model trained on natural images,</font>

88
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:09,760
<font color="#000000">while costing far more compute.</font>

89
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:13,680
<font color="#000000">Yet both approaches deliver a dramatic improvement
over the previous state-of-the-art</font>

90
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:17,600
<font color="#000000">classifier for reading atmospheric phenomena from
ocean radar.</font>

91
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:21,520
<font color="#000000">The authors argue the idea still holds promise, and
that longer training,</font>

92
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:25,440
<font color="#000000">better tuning,</font>

93
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:29,360
<font color="#000000">and pretext tasks designed for remote sensing
deserve exploration before drawing final conclusions.</font>
